We discovered that the code ran on certain servers and not on others (even though the OSes were identical - both Ubuntu 18.04 LTS - and even though the Docker containers where the actual INLA code was run were identical).

We figured out that the origin of the problem was that the servers (KVM virtual machines) on which the containers were running had different virtual cpus configured. This resulted in different supported CPU flags being available on different servers. The old non-functioning virtual machine definition had

  <cpu mode='custom' match='exact'>
    <model fallback='allow'>Opteron_G3</model>
  </cpu>

whereas we left out the cpu definition out in the current (functioning) version.

The list of flags of the current functioning version is

$ lscpu
Architecture:        x86_64
CPU op-mode(s):      32-bit, 64-bit
Byte Order:          Little Endian
CPU(s):              4
On-line CPU(s) list: 0-3
Thread(s) per core:  1
Core(s) per socket:  1
Socket(s):           4
NUMA node(s):        1
Vendor ID:           AuthenticAMD
CPU family:          6
Model:               13
Model name:          QEMU Virtual CPU version 1.5.3
Stepping:            3
CPU MHz:             3199.450
BogoMIPS:            6398.90
Hypervisor vendor:   KVM
Virtualization type: full
L1d cache:           64K
L1i cache:           64K
L2 cache:            512K
NUMA node0 CPU(s):   0-3
Flags:               fpu de pse tsc msr pae mce cx8 apic sep mtrr pge mca cmov pat pse36 clflush mmx fxsr sse sse2 syscall nx lm nopl cpuid tsc_known_freq pni cx16 hypervisor lahf_lm abm sse4a 3dnowprefetch vmmcall

Still not 100% clear which flag is required to avoid the cryptic 'Illegal instruction' error message, but hopefully this helps someone else running into this issue.

Best,
Tobias

