I got this error when trying to project the mesh grid to prediction grid and have no clue how to get over it. Could anyone help?
In my case, m1*m2 = length(pred.grid), Swiss.mesh has a smaller set of grids(as well as coverage) comparing to the pred.grid.

proj.grid <- inla.mesh.projector(Swiss.mesh,
                                 xlim=range(pred.grid[,1]), ylim=range(pred.grid[,2]),
                                 dims=c(m1,m2))
post.mean.pred.grid <- inla.mesh.project(proj.grid, post.mean.pred)
Error in projector$proj$A %*% as.vector(field) :
  Cholmod error 'X and/or Y have wrong dimensions' at file ../MatrixOps/cholmod_sdmult.c, line 90

From uf2baaf6252beabe3 at groups.google.com  Tue Oct 29 16:13:05 2019
From: uf2baaf6252beabe3 at groups.google.com (Julianne Meisner)
Date: Tue, 29 Oct 2019 16:13:05 +0000
Subject: Argument "control.predictor=list(compute=TRUE)" markedly slows computation
Message-ID: <p4mxcXRApXM.5mnciW_dAgAJ@r-inla-discussion-group.groups.google.com>
Archived-At: <https://groups.google.com/g/r-inla-discussion-group/c/p4mxcXRApXM/m/5mnciW_dAgAJ>
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset="utf-8"
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

Hi all,

I'm performing spatial regression so interested in extracting model residuals, in particular to look at residual autocorrelation. I initially forgot the argument "control.predictor=list(compute=TRUE)," and the model in question took about 2 hours to run. Adding this in is really slowing things down-- we're at 23 hours with no results.

Do you have any suggestions of how to speed this up? Or should I just use the linear predictor and random effects? My code is below; "dengue" is a data frame, each row is a district-month (district=ubigeo), and outcome is an indicator variable for whether that district-month experienced a dengue outbreak. I'm working on a remote server (Linux) and have been asked to keep my work to 4 cores (hence the num.threads argument).

Thank you,
Julianne

---------------------------------------------------------------------
formula <- outbreak_bin ~ 1 + nino.binary3.4  + as.factor(region.natural) + bs(dengue$time, knots = (19*4-1)) + f(ubigeo.struct,model="besag",
graph="ENSO.graph") + f(ubigeo.unstruct,model="iid")

N34 <- inla(formula, family="binomial", data=dengue, Ntrials=1, verbose=FALSE, num.threads = inla.getOption("num.threads"), control.predictor=list(compute=TRUE))

